WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
clogged. Okay, so then we'll look up a

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.000
little bit higher and we can find the

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
auricles, and auricle just means ear. And so

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
here is one on this side. This is the

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.000
left ventricle, so this must be the left

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
auricle. You can see it's kind of flappy,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
like an ear, and the auricle is the part

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
of the atrium that flaps out like this.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.000
So there's actually more space inside of

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
the atrium, but the part of it that forms

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
this ear-like shape is called the auricle.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
So if it's pinned from the outside, you

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
say auricle. If the chamber is

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
pinned, then you say atrium. And then you

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
can find the other flap over on this

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
side, so this would be, since this is the

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
right ventricle, then up here this must

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
be the right

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
auricle. The next vessel to find would be

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
the aorta. So if you, this is the

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
pulmonary trunk, and you look right

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
behind it, the large vessel you see

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
coming up should be the aorta, and you

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
can see how strong and muscular that is.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.000
So if you're looking at it from on top,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
there's the pulmonary trunk going down

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
to the right ventricle, and here's the

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
aorta going down behind the pulmonary

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
trunk to the left ventricle. Okay, now

